# FlixPatrol US historical rankings (redis-search)

## Scope

Historical chart storage lives in this repository under `src/api/subapi/flixpatrol/`.
The HTML top10 scraper in `core.py` is unchanged. Rankings use the authenticated
v2 API via `client.py` and `history.py`.

## Backfill

```bash
make backfill-flixpatrol ENV=dev
make backfill-flixpatrol ENV=dev write=1
make backfill-flixpatrol ENV=dev start=2025-01-01 end=2025-01-31 write=1
make backfill-flixpatrol ENV=dev ARGS="--transform-only"
```

`ENV=dev` loads `config/etl.dev.env` and points Redis at `localhost:6381` (IAP tunnel). `ENV=local` loads the FlixPatrol credentials from `config/etl.dev.env`, then applies Redis settings from `config/local.env`. `write=1` stamps `$.flixpatrol` only after the history fetch succeeds.

Direct module invocation (same repo, `PYTHONPATH=src` set by Make):

```bash
python -m api.subapi.flixpatrol.history
python -m api.subapi.flixpatrol.history --start 2025-01-01 --end 2025-01-31
python -m api.subapi.flixpatrol.history --transform-only
```

- Optional `--start` and `--end` are inclusive calendar dates. Default: January 2021
  through today.
- Each month uses `date[type][eq]=4`, full calendar month bounds, and
  `country[eq]=cnt_iMUHNbZvnNHK5YdhgwtOoP4u`. No title, company, or rank filter.
- Pagination follows `links.next` only. Do not send `page`.
- Complete files: `data/flixpatrol/us-monthly/YYYY-MM.json` only after the last page.
- Closed months are not refetched. The open month may refresh.
- Each HTTP GET is cached with prefix `flixpatrol` and method prefix
  `rankings_request`. The cache key is the full URL (including `starting_after[id]`).
  Closed periods: one year TTL. Open period: 24 hours. Do not change the prefix to bust cache.

## Weekly maintenance

- `run_nightly_flixpatrol` in `history.py` runs on Wednesday only.
- Uses `date[type][eq]=3` for missing completed Monday–Sunday US weeks through the
  previous Sunday. First run stores only the latest completed week.
- Week files: `data/flixpatrol/us-weekly/YYYY-MM-DD.json` (Monday date).
- Nightly ETL job `flixpatrol_weekly` in `config/etl_jobs.yaml` calls
  `flixpatrol_nightly_etl`, then stamps Redis from title files.
- The ETL container receives `FLIXPATROL_USERNAME` and `FLIXPATROL_API_KEY`
  from `config/etl.dev.env` through `scripts/deploy_etl_vm.sh` and
  `docker/etl-entrypoint.sh`.

## Title transformer

- `transform_flixpatrol_rankings` writes `data/flixpatrol/us-titles/{mc_id}.json`.
- Rankings `type=1` with `/movie/` → `tmdb_movie_{tmdbId}`. `type=2` with `/tv/` →
  `tmdb_tv_{tmdbId}`. Skip rows with missing `tmdbId` or link/type mismatch.
- `peak_total` is max `valueTotal`, not a sum. Weekly and monthly rows stay separate records.

## API

- `POST /api/flixpatrol` with `mc_type=movie|tv` and `mc_id` or comma-separated `mc_ids`.
- Response shape: `{ "mc_id": "...", "flixpatrol": { ... } | null }`.
- Batch responses are a JSON array in request order.
- Missing Redis document: single request returns HTTP 404; batch items include
  `{ "mc_id", "error", "status_code": 404 }`.
- Missing stored history on an existing document: HTTP 200 with `"flixpatrol": null`
  (media doc exists but `flixpatrol:{mc_id}` sidecar is absent).
- Returns the **full** history object from the sidecar key, including every `records` row.
- Does not call FlixPatrol live and does not return the full media document.

## Redis

- `media:{mc_id}` field `flixpatrol` (not indexed): summary fields plus `records` abridged
  to the first and last chart rows only (search/details payload size control).
- `flixpatrol:{mc_id}` JSON key: complete history object with all `records` (not in
  `idx:media`). Only `POST /api/flixpatrol` reads this key.
- TMDB nightly load copies an existing media `flixpatrol` block forward without rewriting
  the sidecar; title files under `data/flixpatrol/us-titles` or `FLIXPATROL_TITLES_DIR`
  replace the abridged media field and refresh the sidecar when present.
- Bulk stamp: `PYTHONPATH=src python -m etl.flixpatrol_payload` writes both keys.
  Re-run `make backfill-flixpatrol ... write=1` to shrink documents that still carry
  full `records` on the media doc from an earlier stamp.

## Media Manager propagation

The Redis backfill does not automatically enqueue the updated documents in Media
Manager. To propagate existing FlixPatrol metadata without rerunning the fetch:

```bash
make push-flixpatrol-to-mm ENV=local
make push-flixpatrol-to-mm ENV=dev
make push-flixpatrol-to-mm ENV=local ARGS="--dry-run --limit 20"
```

The command scans `media:*`, selects documents with a `flixpatrol` object, applies
the normal Media Manager eligibility filter, and submits eligible documents in
`metadata_only=True` batches. Finalization is enabled by default; use
`ARGS="--no-finalize"` to queue without publishing. `ENV=dev` requires the Redis
IAP tunnel and an authenticated Media Manager proxy running on port 8099:

```bash
gcloud run services proxy media-manager-etl-development \
  --project=media-circle --region=us-central1 --port=8099
```

The Make target verifies that proxy and overrides the protected Cloud Run URL
with `http://127.0.0.1:8099`. `ENV=local` uses the local Media Manager URL.
